== Contextual Inquiry
The contextual inquiry was conducted in collaboration with Brain Stimulation and was used to inform the needs and design decisions of this study's co-design sessions. The contextual inquiry resulted in three main themes: (1) Phases of Therapy, (2) Current System and Technology Benefits & Limitations, and (3) Ideal Digitalized Rehabilitation.

=== Phases of therapy
The general flow of therapy as per the participants *(P3-P5)* from the case study of Brain Stimulation always went through three main phases: (1) Assessment & therapy planning, (2) session level and (3) task level (see figure 1a). The therapy is always initiated with a meeting where both experienced issues and goals are discussed together with the patient. Only then does the therapist proceed to performing van the patient such as DiSTRO cognitive assessment tests and daily activity tests to assess the type of issues the patient has and the severity (Phase 1).

_" I perform cognitive tests and function tests to ensure if it is cognitive issues alone or not. Then ask about family and personal life. Ask about the day so far. Daily chores and activities are checked [e.g. cleaning, cooking, brushing teeth, showering]" *- P5*_

Once the assessment is finished, the therapist then creates a therapy plan with the agreed goals in mind, and creates a session plan (Phase 2) which is most often used throughout the entire therapy due to the limited number of available exergames. During the rehabilitation session (Phase 3), the therapist adjusts game parameters, pauses/continues and changes task/game based on the patient's physical and emotional condition. During the session and task level, the therapist also takes notes regarding what was done and how it went for the patient. These notes are regularly analyzed and compared to track progress and update assessment and therapy plan. 

_"I use the notes to see how the patient is feeling that day and adapt it [session plan & therapy plan] accordingly" *- P3*_

Besides a few notable observations during _session level_ and _task level_ (see figure 1a), all of the findings matched with the observation that was conducted with *P3*. The additional findings were mainly in the form of therapist-patient interaction which seemed to be a necessity for the rehabilitation session to go smoothly. The main interaction was continuos chatting in intervals to keep the patient relaxed and receive feedback to operate from, while giving the patient enough time to focus on their tasks. The feedback poking was everything from when to rest and for how long to rest, to if the task was too easy, too difficult and if they wanted to change to a different task. This feedback was then used to adjust game parameters such as the difficulty, adding objects with extra points to motivate the patient or completely changing task. *Another observation was how the therapist took handwritten notes on paper rather than inputting it in the assessment or exergames tool.* One last observation note was how the therapist configured both before the rehabilitation session and in real-time during the rehabilitation session. This insight was used to introduce temporality as one of the dimensions in the co-design session (see figure X) to determine in which phase of therapy therapists want to pre-configure or configure in real-time, and how the system's agency distribution should be designed to support that. 

*Based off of all the above accumulated findings on the phases of therapy which largely matched with previous research on the therapy process (see section: therapist-facing interface)*, figure 1b was designed to include the final four phases of therapy for the therapist-facing system: (1) therapy planning & goal-setting, (2) session planning, (3) rehabilitation session, and (4) evaluation session. 

=== Current System and Technology Benefits & Limitations
All participants *(P1-P5)* during the contextual inquiry preferred Exergames for personalized rehabilitation, where the main limitations reported were technical related. *As expected*, the general opinion among the participants was that Exergames for rehab is more beneficial as it allows you to instantly review all necessary data *while observing*, making it easier for assessment, decision-making and collaboration with other colleagues. Another repeated but *expected* finding was how Exergames gives the therapist the ability to personalize rehab in various phases while simultaneously appearing more fun and motivating for the patient. The phases mentioned stretched from the ability to create personalized planning to keeping track of and comparing patient data overtime, personalize difficulty accordingly, and appoint various games depending on the problem that a patient has. One participant *(P1)* also highlighted how XR exergames in particular is more effective as it allows the patient to perform high repetitions in simulations that are similar to real-life without being boring like in traditional therapy.

_"[XR] Exergames that are evidence-based and developed with clinical therapy in mind tend to improve patients states" *- P1*_

_"...[Exergames] the strength is the flexibility. The strength is really to be able to personalize" *- P4*_

While the overall perception of technology and Exergames for rehabilitation was positive, connective issues between the therapist-facing interface and the exergame tool in particular was *surprisingly* consistently reported. Although this is not the main focus of this study, it still gives insights into the problems of current systems used for rehabilitation through exergames. Other technical issues related to tools abruptly crashing or updating during usage, leading to affected _rehabilitation sessions_. These technical issues were perceived as highly disruptive as it would result in time consumption, thereby reducing time available for the actual rehabilitation session. This was a major concern for all the participants *(P1-P5)* since the rehabilitation field is already short on time, sometimes only having 20-30 minutes to give to a patient. *Subsequently, interaction cost was included as a probe dimension for the co-design session (see figure X) to be able to determine how agency distribution should be distributed across the four therapy phases (see figure 1b) while being time efficient and effortless.*
