=== Ideal Digitalized Rehabilitation
The participants mentioned the goal of rehabilitation technology is to provide fun and motivating rehabilitation to the patient alongside evidence-based rehabilitation, and to move the entire field towards precision medicine while focusing on the patient for their individual needs. *Access to rehabilitation was also pointed out in how these technologies might enable access to patients in need through remote rehabilitation.* In addition *P3* mentioned “when patient have to travel a lot to come to rehab, they are absolutely exhausted when they come to the rehabilitation” pointing out the value of remote rehabilitation technology. 

The participants mention that technology is not a replacement to therapy but a digital setup of evidence-based training which can be used in every clinic. The participants hoped that these technologies become simpler, like an app as technological development progresses instead of a full kit that requires expert for initial setup. The participants wanted to have a system that had both assessment and therapy in the same system. Participant *P2* specifically, also mentioned how the system should be adaptable to the therapist's workflow and rehabilitation domain: 

_"The product should not be a fixed, but a flexible tool for therapists, tool should be able to be used in many different ways to fit various rehabilitation purposes/preferences." *- P2*_

When asked about interface, the participants wanted more simpler settings and exergame parameters as well as simpler connection between patient technology, and therapists monitoring and control technology. Although, they preferred simple difficulty change over finer controls for exergames, they wanted controls on other attributes of the game to provide individualized care. Lastly, the lack of levels, and grading of levels were also mentioned as a limitation and as an important feature to be able to know where in the difficulty spectrum a patient is and adjust it accordingly.*This finding helped us generate granularity as another dimension for both the pilot study and the co-design session (see figure X).*

*Participants were also asked about agency over settings and exergame parameters between themselves and the system, and interestingly they preferred to have both despite wanting to be in full agency*. One participant *(P1)* clearly illustrated this contradiction through conducted research on self-rehabilitation with minimal therapists supervision which was found to be successful, despite therapists advocating for full control and presence. This intrigued/triggered us to explore the boundaries and conditions in which the therapists would accept system agency. Hence, the dimension human vs. system agency in execution and reasoning (see figure X) was constructed to understand in which of the therapy phases system agency is accepted and beneficial to the therapists. 

== Co-Design Sessions
The co-design sessions were conducted one-on-one with 4 occupational therapists and 1 physiotherapist. The co-design sessions resulted in five main themes: (1) Desired System, (2) Conditional Control (3) The Paradox in Details, (4) Digitalized Rehabilitation Still Necessitate Human Interaction, and (5) Digitalized Rehabilitation is the Future.

=== Desired System (Change title)
All participants *(P4-P8)* were tasked with designing their own system throughout all phases of therapy (see @designsystem) by assigning the scenarios (see @codesign) to each of the four phases: *(1) Therapy Planning & Goal-Setting, (2) Session planning, (3) Rehabilitation Session, and (4) Evaluation Session* (see @phases). The scenarios were: (1) a system with full human agency of the therapeutic process without system support, (2) a system that provides recommendations, (3) a system which requires instructions/inputs by the therapists, and (4) a system with full automation with therapists-on-the-loop. Each participant was informed to use one or more scenario cards as they see fit for each phase. The participants were also informed that they could use the same scenario card for all phases if they desired. 

@systemdesign displays how the participants distributed each of the scenarios among the four phases during the designing of the desired system (See @designsystem). During _the therapy planning & goal-setting phase_, the participants preferred Scenario 1 (Full Human Agency) and Scenario 3 (Prompt System), both of which provided full autonomy and control of decision-making to the therapists without assistance from the system. For _session planning_, therapists preferred assistance from system either for recommendations and clinical reasoning or for execution of the decisions, preferring Scenario 2 (Recommendation System) and Scenario 3 (Prompt System). The results indicate therapists would prefer to automate the actual rehabilitation sessions either partially or fully with Scenario 2 (Recommendation System) and Scenario 4 (Full System Agency) being the most preferred. Similarly, the therapists seem to prefer partial or fully automated system to evaluate and reflect after the session is completed with results indicating preference for Scenario 2 (Recommendation System), Scenario 3 (Prompt System) and Scenario 4 (Full System Agency). 

The figure also displays how many times each scenario was picked in total, where the system that provides recommendation (Scenario 2) was most preferred among the participants, while the fully automated system (Scenario 4) was least preferred. One interesting finding was how Scenario 3 (Prompt System) was picked second most to Scenario 2 (Recommendation System), despite being the most dislikeable during the entirety of the co-design session. 

  _"That [scenario 3] sounds like a lot of work. No, I got tired just of the idea that, Oh my God, no, it's too. That's too much." *- P5*_

