The @systemdesign also suggests that the participants do not want fully automated system for *_therapy planning & goal-setting_ nor in _session planning_* as they need more control in early stages to provide individualized therapy as *P4* describes below:

  _"I like to be in control because every person that I meet is different and every day is different." *- P4*_

During each scenario walk-through, all co-design participants were also asked at which phase they would find a scenario to be most useful before they got to design their own system. Out of the five co-design participants, only two participants *(P4, P7)* resulted in conflicting discrepancies. *P4* had elaborated that Scenario 1 (Full Human Agency) would be the most useful for _session planning_ and _evaluation session_ at first, then changed their mind and picked rehabilitation session. When *P4* however designed their own system, they assigned Scenario 1 (Full Human Agency) to _therapy planning & goal-setting_ and nothing else, despite knowing they could use the same scenario for all phases. *P7* on the other hand had appointed _Scenario 2_ (Recommendation System) to be most useful for _therapy planning & goal-setting_, yet only picked Scenario 3 (Prompt System) for _therapy planning & goal-setting_ when designing the desired system. *P7* also did this despite knowing they could combine several scenarios for the same phase as well. 

=== Conditional Control
The participants mentioned control over therapy was important to them as a therapist but also pointed out when and under which circumstances they were willing to hand over the control over to the system. The preferences were conditional and largely dependent on time pressure, level of trust in the system, type of decision being made, *and longer-term professional outlook.*

The participants emphasized that autonomy over clinical decision-making was integral to being a therapist as every therapy patient and every session is different, and having this control allows them to cater better to the patient needs in that moment. The participants compared having options to adjust therapy parameters to control, make and enact the decision to the feeling of having autonomy and control over therapeutic process.

  _“ I like to be in control so and every person that I meet is different and every day is different.”_ *-P4*

Other participants (P8, P6) didn’t feel comfortable passively accepting system recommendations (Scenario 2) and stressed that they needed to be responsible for the clinical decisions. For *P7*, having full control in the system is similar to their current therapy practice, noting _“would feel like therapist doing therapist work which it is supposed to do”_ while P8, also strongly advocated that clinical-reasoning needs to be led by therapists. Trust in the system was therefore an important precondition to let go of control entirely. This delegation of control was seen as conditional and the threshold for full delegation was high. Trust was described as something built over time and based on the results the system consistently produced.  

  _“You would have to be very comfortable with the system that you knew that it was all that it was equally or better than yourself.”_ *- P6*

Participants also mentioned relying too heavily on the system would reduce their clinical reasoning skills, especially to novice therapists who are still developing their own skills, to a point where it would hinder thinking for themselves. They mentioned clinical reasoning is as much about experience and learning as it is about skill and knowledge. However, when participants were introduced to therapist control alongside automation, they were more accepting of the shared control. The most preferred model for this automation and control was system proposes options and clinicians deciding to accept, adjust or reject them (Scenario 2). The feeling of being in control was more important if the system took a part of either reasoning or execution. 

  _“As long as I can adjust if I want to, of course, then it could be a good alternative.”_ *-P5*

In contrast, three out of the five participants (P4, P6, P8) did not prefer a fully autonomous system (Scenario 4), even if they were able to override therapy parameters. 
They associated full automation with a loss of control over the therapy process and expressed concern about how this would affect their role as a therapists and the patient-therapist relationship. However, two participant (P5, P7) were largely positive about the fully autonomous system (Scenario 4) as long as they could override, elaborating that their work would be easier this way and allow them to focus on patient goals. 

  //Choose one i guess
  //_“I think this might be my least favourite system.. Yeah, it doesn't sit overly well with me. I think, like, there's just no autonomy there.”_ *-P8*
  
  //_"I think it's a scary thing to do. Even if I get access to documentation or summaries or data”_ *-P4*

_“If this was the main therapy, I would have a problem with this ... My opinion wouldn’t change even if I could override things”_ *-P6*

Even across scenarios, the participants agreed on a hybrid pattern of control of pre-planning supported by live adjustments during the session with patient. These adjustments were fine-tuning rather than a complete replanning but were still viewed as important to account for patient fatigue, pain or day-to-day variations. These feeling remained consistent across all scenarios except the fully autonomous system.
  
  _“Every great plan can crumble quite quickly depending on like, say, fatigue, pain.”_ *-P8*

Additionally, similar to the *Contextual Inquiry*, time was again a central factor, this time in regards to how much control participants were willing to let go of. Time was described as a luxury in healthcare that competed with realities of clinical practice with *P8* noting _“time constraint is probably one of our biggest things that stop us from kind of delivering like better therapy”_. Under this time pressure, all participants said they would be willing to accept system's plan and parameters, even if they were imperfect for the moment, rather than interrupt the therapy, if the required time or effort was too high, as long as there was no harm to the patient. 

  _“I would say yes, because I'm human and… sometimes you don't have the time or the effort to do it”_  *- P4 *
  
This trade-off had a clear limit though, as the therapists still prioritized patient’s care if they believed additional time or effort would significantly provide better outcomes for the patient. 
  
  _“No extra control is not worth the time, unless it gives a better outcome”_ *-P7*
 
=== The Paradox in Details
There was an underlying tension on how high the granularity of the information presented by the system or provided to the system should be. Higher degree of detail was simultaneously seen as a route to understand and as a cognitive and temporal burden. However, a clear pattern of preference was noticed for Scenario 1 (Full Human Agency). Each participant *(P4-P8)* presented a clear preference for lesser degree of detail and default interface with less information with the option to access additional information when necessary. They wanted combination of presets with manual adjustments to provide effect individualized rehabilitation.

  _“It would be nice to have some of the features hidden or taken care of by the computer.”_ *- P4*
  
  _“As long as I can see the progression and can make changes that are important or when needed.”_ *- P7*

Similarly, all participants *(P4-P8)* also described they prefer to give instructions to the system (Scenario 3) in less detailed manner focusing on functional instructions rather than fine-grained instructions reasoning higher time and effort required to provide detailed instructions. They also wanted recommendations (Scenario 2) to include enough explanatory details to understand why a suggestion was made, as long as this did not become excessive. This suggests a preference for a balanced design that combines simplicity, focus on important informations, preset prompts, and manual adjustments for more technical experienced users.

  _”In an easy way say it or type it then it would be good.. otherwise you you lose the contact because you still have to be there with the patient”_ *- P5*
  
  _“You might want to wish to improve (the range of motion) but I don't think the system should tell you how much to increase by.”_ *- P8*  

Participants *(P4-P8)* saw detailed output as most useful in moments of disagreement or uncertainty, which required further investigation to check its validity or to learn. When there was a discrepancies between system reasoning and their own, one participant *(P6)* explained “you would probably have to like think about why would it say that why would the system say that of course you would have to make that reflection”. Regardless of the composition of agency between human-machine, the higher degree of granularity was important either to understand why there was difference in reasoning, or to learn why the system provided recommendation or what changes the system made based on their instructions. The participants viewed the high granularity to explain the system’s reasoning and system’s processes as a positive requirement. They mentioned this framing was particularly valuable for novice therapists to learn reasoning behind therapeutic decisions. 
  
  _“would be nice to know the why the recommendation is set.”_ -*(P7)*
  
When the system performed reasoning autonomously (Scenario 2, Scenario 4), participants noted they wanted more details rather than less. One participant *(P8)* noted “I would wanna be seeing the reasoning and whether or not I would agree with it. I think the level of detail as to why would be very important.” The participants were interested in understanding why behind the reasoning, however, the level of detail they preferred were still more diagnostic as *P6* mentioned “_Like it would have to explain the reason very clearly, what kind of cognitive ability is lacking, severity and why, because there are many different cognitive functions._”, rather than fine-level parameters as *P5* notes “_but not too specific like 3cm, I'm not that data interested._”

Overall, participants did not want less information but more curated information. High degree of detail felt like a burden when required as input or displayed by default, but was however preferred when it was required for justification, served as a means for reflection or surfaced something the participants might have missed. 


=== Digitalized Rehabilitation Still Necessitate Human Interaction
Another main theme that repeatedly arose throughout the entirety of the co-design session and across all above themes was the importance of a good patient-therapist relationship. All co-design participants *(P4-P8)* asserted that despite the benefits of digitalized rehabilitation through exergames, it still does not cover all the necessities for a successful rehabilitation. The two most significant necessities for successful rehabilitation which technology alone can not cover for are, (1) accounting for the remaining hours of a patient's day, and (2) making the patient feel seen, heard and understood throughout the rehabilitation process. 

_"Rehabilitation process is not always linear that goes from progress to progress. So the contradiction is not unexpected. Because you're going to have dips." *- P5*_

This became even more conclusive when all participants stated they would rather see the patient over additional quantitative data enabled through technology. The participants argued that not having access to observe the patient, meant the therapist could not give or receive feedback, and therefore not be able to take patient's requests and state of mind into account. The participants emphasis on human connection and its impact on the therapy was well articulated by *P8*:

  _"Digitalized therapy can be like an adjunct, it can't fully take away from what we do, that kind of patient connection and the utilization of clinical reasoning and skills on site."_ *- P8*

One participant in particular *(P6)* also illuminated the issue of not being able to determine whether the patient had actually done the assigned exercises and improved, or had another person do the exercises for them if they were to never meet the patient for checkups. The participant further elaborated that this could increase the chances of incorrect assessment which could lead to detrimental consequences for both the patient and the therapist. Regardless, the participant could still see the potential of some remote rehabilitation with more infrequent meetings as: 

  _"it could be a good option for somebody that doesn't can't see an occupational therapy regularly or have to travel a very long time to see one"_ *- P6*

All of the above mentioned reasoning were persistently connected to how essential they were to establishing better motivation and trust between the patient and the therapist, thus increasing the likelihood of consistent rehabilitation and correct clinical assessment. This difficulty is clearly illuminated below:

  _"From my experience I would say the hardest thing would be to get the patient to actually do the rehabilitation"_ *- P7*

There were however some contextual situations where the participants *(P4-P8)* could consider not seeing the patient at all. Those were only if (1) the system was ethical and secure for remote use and if the patient would agree to being recorded for the purpose of observation and feedback, (2) whether or not the patient is in need of frequent feedback, further emphasizing the importance of not losing the human interaction in rehabilitation and (3) trust that the system would provide effective rehabilitation.

=== Digitalized Rehabilitation is the Future

The participants were uncertain whether the type of medium for digitalized rehabilitation would affect the workflow in their therapeutic process, all participants agreed digitalized rehabilitation is the future. One participant *(P4)* with 25 years of experience as an OT illuminated the change in attitude towards digital therapy: 

"As an OT, when I started, it was frowned upon, and . . . now, everyone is doing computers or tablets . . . it's a huge part of the activities that we do every day" . *- P4*

While every participant agreed that we are moving towards digital therapy, the level of digitalization varied from simple digitalized assessment *(P5)* to fully automated therapy system *(P4, P6)*. *P6* hoped that the access to rehabilitation for people who wouldn’t have otherwise received is one of the outcome of more technologically advanced rehabilitation system while P8 hoped that the technology is more helpful than harmful and that we do not lose our patient-centeredness because of it. 

  _“I don’t want technology to take away from the humanization of what we do, but it could definitely assist us in being more thoughtful to aid that clinical reasoning and help us be more efficient.” *- P8*_

Regardless of the participants positive outlook on digitalized rehabilitation, three of the participants still mentioned a few concerns. *P6* noted that the technology has to be at least similar if not better than current process to shift towards a new technology. In contrast, *P5* was concerned with the impending stress when technology does not function as intended. *P7* on the other hand noted the time consuming learning curve required to learn the new technology. 

The preferences for future system interactions and technology mediums also varied among the participants. Participants showed clear preference towards traditional system interaction methods like form-based interactions (e.g writing, select input, buttons etc.) for planning therapy and sessions. When prompted about voice interaction, their concerns were more technical like noisy environment, voice recognition, accents and privacy rather than ease of use. While the preferences for technology mediums stretched from Wii-like technology with screen and motion sensors to XR glasses, they always preferred technology mediums they were already familiar with and in alignment with their respective therapeutic process. Despite that, tablet, was the main medium that was repeatedly mentioned with one exception, by all participants *(P4, P6-P8)* as they are both familiar, easy to carry around and supports their workflow. This is given the fact they often have to move between rooms and may have to care for multiple patients at a time. 

  _“Something more portable. Since I have to go room to room” *- P5*_

On the other hand, participant *P4* emphasized that the quality of the data and accuracy is more important than the specific technology used. Similarly, two participants *(P6, P8)* mentioned they would like to see other assessment data and the ability of comparing current patient data with standardized performance from similar patients to make better therapeutic decisions. 






